  "findings": [
   "knee at 3000 to 3090 MHz: above it 1 % of fps buys 5 to 7 % of power in TSE GT2 (5 to 8 % across the three tests); from 3000 down to 2400 MHz 1 % of fps saves about 1.5 % of power",
   "the voltage the card settles at on a flat cap is not set by the cap: TSE GT2 read 0.95, 0.95, 0.93, 0.95, 0.98, 0.985, 0.91 V from 3000 down to 2400 MHz",
   "so the steps are not monotonic: 2500 MHz drew more than 2600 MHz in all three tests",
   "a lower cap is therefore not a true undervolt"
  ],
  "voltage_note": "core_voltage_v only counts logger samples above 400 W; TSE GT1 at the lower caps draws less than that most of the time, so its voltage values below flat_2800 rest on few samples (flat_2400: none) and should not be used"
 },
